# 233: overflow inside a recursive function is a `Value` error on the `*` — "not
# a wrap and not a panic" (§4), and §6 `.41` requires `checked_*`. fact(21) is
# 21 * 20!, and 20! is already 2432902008176640000 against an i64 max of
# 9223372036854775807, so the OUTERMOST multiply is the one that overflows,
# after 20 invocations have already returned successfully.
# Every invocation's `*` is the same AST node on line 3, so the reported line is
# 3 whichever multiply an engine blames. The first print shows the recursion
# itself is sound right up to the edge.
--- source
fn fact(n) {
  if n < 2 { return 1; }
  return n * fact(n - 1);
}
print fact(20);
print fact(21);
--- expect
2432902008176640000
error: Value at line 3: integer overflow
